12. TCP — Transmission Control Protocol
TCP means:

Transmission Control Protocol
TCP provides reliable, ordered byte-stream delivery between applications.

Simplified example:

Sender

Packet/Data 1
Packet/Data 2
Packet/Data 3   ---> Lost
Packet/Data 4
Packet/Data 5
TCP detects that required data has not been successfully received and can retransmit it.

Packet/Data 3 ---> Retransmitted
The receiving application can ultimately receive the complete ordered data stream.

TCP is used when reliability and ordered delivery are important.

Examples include:

HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2
HTTPS over TCP
SSH
Database connections
Note:

HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP rather than TCP.
13. UDP — User Datagram Protocol
UDP means:

User Datagram Protocol
UDP is connectionless at the transport layer.

Simplified example:

Datagram 1
Datagram 2
Datagram 3 ---> Lost
Datagram 4
Datagram 5
UDP itself does not provide TCP-style retransmission and ordered delivery.

If an application requires those features while using UDP, they must be provided at a higher layer/protocol.

UDP is useful where:

Low latency is important
Some packet loss may be acceptable
The application/protocol handles reliability itself
Examples include:

DNS
Streaming use cases
VoIP
Gaming
QUIC / HTTP/3
14. TCP vs UDP
TCP	UDP
Connection-oriented	Connectionless transport
Reliable delivery mechanisms	No built-in reliable delivery
Ordered byte stream	Datagram-based
Retransmission supported	No built-in retransmission
More protocol overhead	Lower protocol overhead
SSH uses TCP	DNS commonly uses UDP
